UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 13, Lesson 1: Everyday foods and sometimes foods', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Everyday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and energy. Sometimes foods, such as chips, lollies, soft drink and cake, are often high in added sugar, salt or saturated fat and low in nutrients, so they are eaten less often and in small amounts. Celebrations and treats are part of life; the aim is balance, not guilt.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort these foods into everyday or sometimes: apple slices, lollies, plain popcorn, sausage roll, milk. Give a reason for the sausage roll.\n\nFEEDBACK\nEveryday: apple slices, plain popcorn, milk. Sometimes: lollies and sausage roll (pastry and processed meat have a lot of saturated fat and salt). Plain popcorn without added butter or sugar is a grain food.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a two-column chart with five everyday and five sometimes foods that your family eats. Write the reason for each choice (nutrients, sugar, salt or fat) and give two ideas for a healthy party food. Do not judge anyone''s food choices.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Everyday foods 