s are never allowed.. Correction: Everyday: apple slices, plain popcorn, milk.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w13-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise everyday foods.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use everyday foods with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify sometimes foods and explain why it matters.","I can practise balance independently on a new prompt.","I can notice nutrients needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Everyday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and energy.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","Everyday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and energy"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about everyday foods with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample. Everyday: wholegrain crackers with cheese, carrot sticks, a banana, water. Sometimes: a birthday cupcake, a packet of chips. Tia enjoys the cupcake at her party and chooses fruit and water at snack time, so her week stays balanced.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort these foods into everyday or sometimes: apple slices, lollies, plain popcorn, sausage roll, milk. Give a reason for the sausage roll.\n\nFEEDBACK\nEveryday: apple slices, plain popcorn, milk. Sometimes: lollies and sausage roll (pastry and processed meat have a lot of saturated fat and salt). Plain popcorn without added butter or sugar is a grain food.","explain":"Everyday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and energy. Sometimes foods, such as chips, lollies, soft drink and cake, are often high in added sugar, salt or saturated fat and low in nutrients, so they are eaten less often and in small amounts. Celebrations and treats are part of life; the aim is balance, not guilt.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Everyday and sometimes foods.","caption":"Balance over the week.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Everyday</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">fruit, vegetables, milk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sometimes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lollies, chips, cake</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters 