ith clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts. Here is a more helpful example: Example. Everyday: wholegrain crackers with cheese, carrot sticks, a banana, water. Sometimes: a birthday cupcake, a packet of chips. Tia enjoys the cupcake at her party and chooses fruit and water at snack time, so her week stays balanced.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nEveryday: apple slices, plain popcorn, milk. Sometimes: lollies and sausage roll (pastry and processed meat have a lot of saturated fat and salt). Plain popcorn without added butter or sugar is a grain food.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about everyday foods with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEveryday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and energy. Sometimes foods, such as chips, lollies, soft drink and cake, are often high in added sugar, salt or saturated fat and low in nutrients, so they are eaten less often and in small amounts. Celebrations and treats are part of life; the aim is balance, not guilt. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than